## Windows (WSL2, experimental)

RomM can run on Windows 10 2004+ and Windows 11 (x64) as a WSL2 distro, without Docker Desktop. Download both files into the same folder:

| File | SHA-256 |
| --- | --- |
| `RomM-@VERSION@.ps1` | `@PS1_SHA256@` |
| `romm-wsl-@VERSION@.tar.gz` | `@ROOTFS_SHA256@` |

**First install**

1. If WSL isn't set up yet, run `wsl --install --no-distribution` in PowerShell **as administrator**, then restart Windows.
2. In a normal PowerShell window in the download folder, using your own ROM folder for `-Library`:

   ```powershell
   Unblock-File .\RomM-@VERSION@.ps1
   powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File .\RomM-@VERSION@.ps1 install -Library 'D:\Games\ROMs'
   powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File .\RomM-@VERSION@.ps1 open
   ```

   RomM opens at `http://localhost:8080`. If that port is taken, add `-Port 8090` to `install`. The library folder follows the [folder structure](https://docs.romm.app/latest/Getting-Started/Folder-Structure/) and is mounted in place, never copied.

**Updating from an earlier version**

```powershell
Unblock-File .\RomM-@VERSION@.ps1
powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File .\RomM-@VERSION@.ps1 upgrade
```

`upgrade` keeps your database, saves, states, covers and settings. Use the new script for `start`, `stop`, `status` and `logs` from then on. Settings live in `%LOCALAPPDATA%\RomM\romm.env`.

To check the downloads came from this repository's release workflow: `gh attestation verify romm-wsl-@VERSION@.tar.gz --repo rommapp/romm`.

